"Behind the eardrum is a tiny space filled with air, and in it are the three smallest bones in your body: the hammer, the anvil, and the stirrup. They pass the shaking along like a lever and make it about twenty times stronger. The stirrup is only about 3 millimeters long, smaller than a grain of rice. A thin tube connects this space to the back of your nose. When your ears pop in an elevator or on an airplane, that tube has just opened to let air in or out.": "43fe365c0f5a",
"Can you hear the rain on the roof?": "1b889b784983",
"Clap your hands. The clap pushes the air, and the push travels outward in waves at about 340 meters per second, fast enough to cross a soccer field in a third of a second. When the waves reach you, the air in your ear shakes back and forth, and your ears turn that shaking into sound. Fast shaking sounds high, like a bird. Slow shaking sounds low, like a drum. Scientists count the shakes in hertz: one hertz means one shake every second. Young people can hear from about 20 to 20,000 hertz.": "eb9f2235b2e1",
"Clap your hands. The clap pushes the air, and the push travels outward in waves at about 340 meters per second, fast enough to cross a soccer field in a third of a second. When the waves reach you, the air in your ear shakes back and forth, and your ears turn that shaking into sound. Fast shaking sounds high, like a bird. Slow shaking sounds low, like a drum. Scientists count the shakes in hertz: one hertz means one shake every second. Young people can hear from about 20 to 20,000 hertz. The part of your ear that you can see is shaped to catch sound and send it into the ear canal, a tube about 2.5 centimeters long. At the end of the canal is the eardrum, a thin, tight skin about as wide as your little fingernail. When sound waves hit it, the eardrum shakes, just like the skin of a drum. The canal also makes earwax, which traps dust and germs and slowly moves out on its own. That is why doctors say you should never push cotton swabs into your ears. Behind the eardrum is a tiny space filled with air, and in it are the three smallest bones in your body: the hammer, the anvil, and the stirrup. They pass the shaking along like a lever and make it about twenty times stronger. The stirrup is only about 3 millimeters long, smaller than a grain of rice. A thin tube connects this space to the back of your nose. When your ears pop in an elevator or on an airplane, that tube has just opened to let air in or out. The stirrup pushes on the cochlea, a tube rolled up like a snail shell and filled with liquid. Inside are about 15,000 hair cells, each topped with tiny hairs that bend when the liquid moves. High notes bend the hairs near the entrance of the cochlea, and low notes travel all the way to its tip. The bending makes nerve signals, which race along the hearing nerve to your brain. Each ear sends signals to both sides of the brain, and by comparing your two ears, your brain can tell which direction a sound came from. Next to the cochlea are three loops filled with liquid, called the semicircular canals. They do not hear at all. Instead, they help you keep your balance. When you turn your head, the liquid in the loops sloshes and bends more hair cells, telling your brain which way you moved. If you spin around and stop suddenly, the liquid keeps swirling for a moment, so your brain thinks you are still turning. That is why you feel dizzy. Hair cells cannot grow back once they are damaged, so loud sounds can harm your hearing for life. Loudness is measured in decibels. Talking is about 60 decibels, and busy traffic is about 85. Sounds that loud or louder can harm your hearing if you listen for a long time. Firecrackers at a temple festival can be louder than 120 decibels, so cover your ears or keep your distance. With earphones, keep the volume low and take breaks. Now try the hearing test below the model and find the highest note you can hear.": "ca17b41a54f6",
"Hair cells cannot grow back once they are damaged, so loud sounds can harm your hearing for life. Loudness is measured in decibels. Talking is about 60 decibels, and busy traffic is about 85. Sounds that loud or louder can harm your hearing if you listen for a long time. Firecrackers at a temple festival can be louder than 120 decibels, so cover your ears or keep your distance. With earphones, keep the volume low and take breaks. Now try the hearing test below the model and find the highest note you can hear.": "a8fbef8a53f1",
